Give me the commodity price information within this image. Give me the response in a plain json object structured as defined in this example

{
    "commodity_prices": [
        {
            "commodity_name": "DIAMOND",
            "available_SCU_quantity": 293,
            "inventory_state": "MEDIUM INVENTORY",
            "price_per_unit": 3984,
            "multiplier": null,
        },
        {
            "commodity_name": "CHLORINE",
            "available_SCU_quantity": 5000,
            "inventory_state": "MAX INVENTORY",
            "price_per_unit": 130,
            "multiplier": null,
        },
        {
            "commodity_name": "CHLORINE",
            "available_SCU_quantity": 5000,
            "inventory_state": "VERY HIGH INVENTORY",
            "price_per_unit": 1.54224230,
            "multiplier": "M",
        }
    ]
}

Additionally, do not nest the commodity name as a key. Provide an array of objects under "commodity_prices", each with:
- "commodity_name": string
- "available_SCU_quantity": integer (use 0 if unknown)
- "inventory_state": one of ["MAX INVENTORY", "VERY HIGH INVENTORY", "HIGH INVENTORY", "MEDIUM INVENTORY", "LOW INVENTORY", "VERY LOW INVENTORY", "OUT OF STOCK"]
- "price_per_unit": number as shown after normalizing separators. If no multiplier is shown, return an integer aUEC value with no decimal places (for example, read "3,984" as 3984). If a "K" or "M" multiplier is shown, decimal places are allowed and the decimal separator must be a dot (for example, read "3.984 K" as price_per_unit 3.984 and multiplier "K").
- "multiplier": "K", "M", or null if the screen explicitly shows a multiplier. Do not infer a multiplier from a thousands comma. A comma in a non-multiplied price is a thousands separator.

Provide the json within markdown ```json ... ```. If you are unable to process the image, just return 'error' as response.
